@interact
def app(step = selector(["HomePage", "Introduction", "Definitions", "Comparison", "Accumulation of Error", "Conclusion"], label="Navigate:")):

    if step == "HomePage":

        show(html("""
        <div style='max-width:2000px; margin:auto; font-family:"Courier";'>

            <h1 style='color: darkblue; text-align:center'>Welcome!</h1>

            <h2 style='text-align:center;'>
            An Analysis of Euler’s Method and Runge-Kutta 4th Order.
            </h2>

            <h3>Project description and objective:</h3>

            <p style='font-size:18px; line-height:1.0;'>
            In this project, I will use concepts from Differential equations to explain two key mathematical
            numerical methods used to approximate solutions to Ordinary Linear Differential Equations.
            The first, used by students at the introductory level, is <b style='color: orange;'>Euler’s Method</b>. 
            The second, which is most commonly used for reasons of accuracy and simplicity, is <b style='color: turquoise;'>Runge-Kutta 4th Order</b>, stemming
            from the genera method of Runge Kutta.
            </p>

            <p style='font-size:18px; line-height:1.0;'>
            These methods are relevant because they are not only used in technical setting such as in Engineering or statistical modeling, but they also represent 
            most notably real world systems of population growth.
            </p>
            
            <h4>Table of contents: (use slider bar at the top of the page)</h4>
            <ol style='font-size:18px; line-height:1.3;'>
                <b><li>
                Introduction </b>- An introduction to our problem and its selevance.
                <b><li>
                Definitions </b>- Defining the methods with reasoning and examples.
                <b><li>
                Comparison </b>- A comparison of both methods, determining which is "best".
                <b><li>
                Accumulation of Error </b>- Stemming from the comparison of the methods, comparing their accumulation of errors.
                <b><li>
                Conclusion </b>- Wrapping up all our findings.
            </ol>

        </div>
        """))


    elif step == "Introduction":
        show(html(r"""
        <div style='max-width:2000px; margin:auto; font-family:"Courier";'>
        <h1 style='color: Black; text-align:center'>An introduction to Methods Of Differential Equation Approximations</h1>
        <h2 'style= font-size = 5px '>What is an Ordinary Differential Equation?</h2>
        <p>
        Ordinary differential equations are equations are able to explain how a quantity 
        changes over time. If they are linear they can be solved using integration or 
        algebraic manipulation, but it is often the case that they are not. For nonlinear
        equations, solutions aren’t fixed and we have to rely on different methods to approximate a solution. 
        This is where we have to rely on two methods, namely Euler's Method and Runge Kutta. 
        We will mainly discuss differential equations of the form \[Y'(x)=f(x, Y(x)),x >= x_0\] where 
        \[Y(x)\] is the function we seek. 
        </p>
        <p>
        The following is an example of a simple differential and its general solution. 
        \[Y'(x)=sin(x)\] is the differential equation whose solution is \[Y(x)=-cos(x)+c\]
        Notice that to solve for the general solution all we have to do is sparate the variables, integrate both sides of the equation, and simplify.
        \[ \frac{dy}{dx}= sin(x)\]
        \[dy = sin(x)dx\]
        \[\int dy=\int \sin(x)\,dx\]
        \[Y(x)=-cos(x)+c\]
        </p>
</div>
"""))
        x = var('x')
        y = function('y')(x)

        DE = diff(y, x) - sin(x)
        gen_sol = desolve(DE, y)

        p1 = plot(gen_sol.subs(_C=1), (x, -7, 7), color='magenta')
        label1 = text(r"$Y(x) = -\cos(x) + 1$", (1.7, 0.5), color='magenta')
        p2 = plot(gen_sol.subs(_C=2), (x, -7, 7), color='blue')
        label2 = text(r"$Y(x) = -\cos(x) + 2$", (1.7, 1.6), color='blue')
        p3 = plot(gen_sol.subs(_C=3), (x, -7, 7), color='green')
        label3 = text(r"$Y(x) = -\cos(x) + 3$", (1.7, 2.6), color='green')

        show(html(r"""
        <p style='max-width:2000px; margin:auto; font-family:"Courier";'> 
        The following graph displays our general solution with three different 
        defined <b>constants of integration</b>. Notice that neither the period or 
        amplitude of the function change but it shifts upwards depending on its 
        constant of integration and to the side when we add the negative leading 
        coefficient to the trigonometric cunction.
        </p>
        """))
        show(p1 + p2 + p3 + label1 + label2 + label3)
        
 
    elif step == "Definitions":
        show("📘 Definitions page")

    
    